---
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
---

<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<meta charset="UTF-8" />
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
		<link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="/favicon.png" />
		<title>In the mountains we are all free — Olivia Fjord Sloth</title>
		<meta name="description" content="A journey to climb Mount Damavand alone, and the people encountered along the way in Iran." />
	</head>

	<body>

		<SiteHeader />

		<main>

			<a class="back" href="/stories">
				← Back to stories
			</a>


			<section class="article-header">

				<p class="eyebrow">
					IRAN · 2022
				</p>

				<h1>
					In the mountains we are all free
				</h1>

				<p class="subtitle">
					I went to Iran to climb its highest mountain alone.
					What stayed with me were the people I met along the way.
				</p>

			</section>


			<section class="hero-image">

				<img
					src="/stories/damavand.webp"
					alt="Mount Damavand in Iran"
					decoding="async"
				/>

			</section>


			<article>

				<p>
					In 2022, I decided to go to Iran to climb Damavand,
					the highest mountain in Iran and the highest volcano in Asia.
				</p>

				<p class="short">
					Alone.
				</p>

				<p>
					I was 30, I had never been to the Middle East before,
					and I had never climbed anything close to 5,610 metres.
				</p>

				<p>
					A lot of people told me not to go.
				</p>

				<p class="short">
					Which of course made me even more curious.
				</p>

				<p>
					I did have my own doubts. Was travelling to Iran somehow
					supporting the Islamic regime? Was going there alone actually
					a good idea?
				</p>

				<p>
					But as a good physics PhD student, I was simply too curious
					not to find out for myself.
				</p>

				<p>
					Because of sanctions, my Danish bank cards wouldn't work in Iran,
					and neither would my Danish SIM card. So before leaving, I withdrew
					a lot of cash, went to a Middle Eastern shop in Copenhagen and bought
					some clothes and a headscarf, and spent hours on YouTube trying to
					learn how to wear it.
				</p>


				<h2>Are you sure you're going to Tehran?</h2>

				<p>
					At Istanbul Airport, it slowly started to dawn on me that maybe
					what I was doing wasn't completely normal.
				</p>

				<p>
					The staff kept insisting that I was going the wrong way.
				</p>

				<blockquote>
					“Are you sure you're going to Tehran?”
				</blockquote>

				<p>
					Yes.
				</p>

				<p>
					After an extra thorough check, I eventually got on the plane.
					I seemed to be the only Western woman travelling alone.
				</p>

				<p>
					That was when I got really nervous.
				</p>

				<p class="short">
					I felt like I was going to throw up.
				</p>

				<p>
					Luckily, there was a young, handsome Iranian guy sitting close
					to me who kept smiling kindly at me. Apparently that was enough
					to stop me from running off the plane again.
				</p>

				<p>
					But I remember having this little voice in my head asking whether
					maybe, this time, I had gone a bit too far with my solo adventures.
				</p>

				<p>
					I arrived in Tehran in the middle of the night. The handsome guy
					from the plane helped me find passport control, and afterwards
					I got help buying a local SIM card.
				</p>

				<p>
					The next day, I was supposed to meet an Iranian couple who had replied
					to a Facebook post I had made because I was trying to figure out where
					to find camping gas.
				</p>

				<p>
					First, though, I had around ten hours to wait at the airport.
				</p>

				<p>
					Somewhere during those ten hours I started to relax.
				</p>

				<p class="short">
					Also, at that point, there wasn't really any way back.
				</p>


				<h2>The Iranian hippies</h2>

				<p>
					Eventually, the couple picked me up.
				</p>

				<p>
					We drove north in an old car that broke down once on the way.
					It seemed fairly fragile, but somehow it held together.
				</p>

				<p>
					The couple I stayed with were Iranian hippies who tried to live
					as self-sufficiently as possible. This included growing their own hash.
				</p>

				<p>
					We had a lot of conversations about Iran and the rest of the world.
				</p>

				<p>
					They were against the Islamic regime, but they were also critical
					of the US and Europe. They didn't dream of Iran becoming Western.
					They wanted their own Iranian way.
				</p>

				<p>
					After a lot of history lessons, good food, some acclimatisation
					for me and a few joints, I left for Damavand.
				</p>


				<h2>In the mountains we are all free</h2>

				<p>
					I had never climbed anything this high before.
					Definitely not alone.
				</p>

				<p>
					I had packed food for seven or eight days because Google hadn't
					been particularly helpful in telling me how long this was actually
					going to take.
				</p>

				<p>
					When I arrived at basecamp, I met two women and a man.
				</p>

				<p>
					One of the women spoke a little English, and one of the first things
					she said to me was:
				</p>

				<blockquote>
					“Take it off! Take it off!”
				</blockquote>

				<p>
					She was pointing at the headscarf covering my hair.
				</p>

				<blockquote>
					“In the mountains we are all free.”
				</blockquote>

				<p>
					So I took it off.
				</p>

				<p>
					I followed them up to the camp at around 4,000 metres.
					There was a big mountain hut where they were staying together
					with lots of other Iranian climbers. I pitched my tent nearby.
				</p>

				<p>
					I had carried all this food up the mountain, but I barely got
					the chance to eat any of it because everyone kept sharing their
					food with me.
				</p>

				<p>
					Some people drank alcohol. Some women wore headscarves.
					Some people prayed. Others didn't.
				</p>

				<p>
					Somehow, up there, all of that seemed less important.
				</p>

				<p class="short">
					We were all just on the same mountain.
				</p>

				<p>
					That night was incredibly cold, and I started to feel the altitude.
				</p>

				<p>
					Still, I decided to continue towards the summit with the three
					of them the next morning, even though another day of acclimatisation
					probably would have been the sensible choice.
				</p>

				<p>
					We got up at 3 am.
				</p>

				<p>
					There were a lot of vertical metres ahead of us.
				</p>

				<p class="short">
					It was not easy.
				</p>

				<p>
					The altitude hit us differently, and at some point I became so cold
					from waiting that I had to leave the group and continue on my own.
				</p>

				<p>
					Later, I caught up with a large group of Iranian men.
				</p>

				<p>
					None of them spoke English.
				</p>

				<p>
					I didn't speak Persian.
				</p>

				<p>
					But they were extremely happy to have me in their group,
					so I joined them and we continued towards the summit together.
				</p>

				<p>
					We reached the top.
				</p>

				<p class="big-line">
					5,610 metres.
				</p>

				<p>
					And everyone made it safely back down.
				</p>


				<h2>The woman on the train</h2>

				<p>
					After recovering from the mountain, I went back to stay with
					the hippie couple for a bit. Then I hitchhiked to Tehran and spent
					another ten days travelling around Iran alone.
				</p>

				<p>
					I met up with the handsome guy from the plane again.
				</p>

				<p>
					And I started meeting people who changed the way I thought about
					the country.
				</p>

				<p>
					Several times when I took the train, a woman would sit down next
					to me and start talking to me.
				</p>

				<p>
					They didn't ask for pity. There was actually a lot of pride
					in the way they spoke about Iran.
				</p>

				<p>
					But they wanted me to understand something.
				</p>

				<blockquote>
					“We are not the Islamic Republic.”
				</blockquote>

				<p>
					One woman told me that her biggest dream was to be allowed
					to play the flute in public.
				</p>

				<p>
					She taught flute in secret.
				</p>

				<p>
					She asked me to go home and tell people about it.
				</p>

				<p>
					I met another young woman who dreamed of becoming a tattoo artist
					and asked if I wanted to take LSD with her.
				</p>

				<p>
					And again and again I met people who invited me into their homes,
					helped me, fed me and shared parts of their lives with me.
				</p>

				<p>
					I had arrived in Iran wondering if maybe everyone back home
					had been right.
				</p>

				<p>
					Maybe I was naive.
				</p>

				<p>
					Maybe travelling there alone was a terrible idea.
				</p>

				<p>
					But when I think about that trip now, those aren't the things
					I remember.
				</p>

				<p class="short">
					I remember the people.
				</p>

				<p>
					The couple who picked up a complete stranger from the airport
					because she needed camping gas.
				</p>

				<p>
					The food that kept appearing in front of me on Damavand even
					though I had brought enough for a week.
				</p>

				<p>
					A group of men I couldn't speak to, but somehow climbed a
					5,610 metre mountain with.
				</p>

				<p>
					Women on trains who wanted me to understand something about
					their country that they felt wasn't reaching the outside world.
				</p>

				<p>
					And I remember standing on a mountain with a woman I had just met,
					who pointed at my headscarf and told me to take it off.
				</p>

				<blockquote class="final-quote">
					“In the mountains we are all free.”
				</blockquote>

			</article>


			<section class="article-end">

				<a href="/stories">
					← More stories
				</a>

			</section>

		</main>

		<SiteFooter />
	</body>
</html>


<style>

	* {
		box-sizing: border-box;
	}

	html,
	body {
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
		background: #f4f1ea;
		color: #171717;
	}

	body {
		font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
	}

	a {
		color: inherit;
		text-decoration: none;
	}


	/* HEADER */

	header {
		width: 100%;
		padding: 30px 48px;

		display: flex;
		justify-content: space-between;
		align-items: center;
	}

	.name {
		font-size: 22px;
		font-weight: 700;
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
	}

	nav {
		display: flex;
		gap: 28px;
	}

	nav a {
		font-size: 15px;
	}

	nav .active {
		border-bottom: 1px solid #171717;
	}


	/* MAIN */

	main {
		max-width: 1500px;
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: 60px 48px 120px;
	}

	.back {
		display: inline-block;
		margin-bottom: 90px;
		font-size: 14px;
	}


	/* HEADER */

	.article-header {
		max-width: 1100px;
		margin-bottom: 70px;
	}

	.eyebrow {
		margin: 0 0 20px;

		font-size: 11px;
		font-weight: 700;
		letter-spacing: 0.18em;
	}

	h1 {
		max-width: 1100px;
		margin: 0;

		font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
		font-size: clamp(65px, 8vw, 125px);
		font-weight: 400;
		line-height: 0.9;
		letter-spacing: -0.045em;
	}

	.subtitle {
		max-width: 800px;
		margin: 45px 0 0;

		font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
		font-size: clamp(25px, 2.8vw, 38px);
		line-height: 1.3;

		color: #555;
	}


	/* HERO IMAGE */

	.hero-image {
		width: 100%;
		margin-bottom: 100px;
	}

	.hero-image img {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		max-height: 800px;

		object-fit: cover;
	}


	/* ARTICLE */

	article {
		max-width: 720px;
		margin: 0 auto;

		font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
		font-size: 21px;
		line-height: 1.75;
	}

	article p {
		margin: 0 0 34px;
	}

	article .short {
		font-size: 25px;
		line-height: 1.4;
	}

	article h2 {
		margin: 95px 0 35px;

		font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
		font-size: clamp(36px, 4vw, 52px);
		font-weight: 400;
		line-height: 1.05;
		letter-spacing: -0.02em;
	}

	blockquote {
		margin: 65px -80px;

		font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
		font-size: clamp(30px, 3.5vw, 46px);
		font-style: italic;
		line-height: 1.2;
	}

	.big-line {
		margin: 55px 0 !important;

		font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
		font-size: clamp(42px, 5vw, 64px);
		line-height: 1.1;
	}

	.final-quote {
		margin-top: 90px;
	}


	/* END */

	.article-end {
		max-width: 720px;
		margin: 110px auto 0;
		padding-top: 35px;

		border-top: 1px solid #bbb;
	}

	.article-end a {
		font-weight: 700;
	}


	/* MOBILE */

	@media (max-width: 800px) {

		header {
			padding: 24px 20px;
		}

		.name {
			font-size: 17px;
		}

		nav {
			gap: 12px;
		}

		nav a {
			font-size: 12px;
		}


		main {
			padding: 50px 20px 90px;
		}

		.back {
			margin-bottom: 60px;
		}


		h1 {
			font-size: 58px;
		}

		.subtitle {
			margin-top: 32px;
			font-size: 25px;
		}


		.hero-image {
			margin-bottom: 65px;
		}

		.hero-image img {
			min-height: 340px;
			object-fit: cover;
		}


		article {
			font-size: 19px;
			line-height: 1.7;
		}

		article .short {
			font-size: 22px;
		}

		article h2 {
			margin-top: 70px;
			font-size: 36px;
		}


		blockquote {
			margin: 55px 0;

			font-size: 30px;
		}


		.big-line {
			font-size: 44px;
		}

	}

</style>
